<%# Trading floor.

    The header used to be hand-rolled, and it carried the whole "My earnings to invest" card - a
    217x114 bordered .tw-card - in the row beside the h1. Measured at 1366x768 that made the header
    row 130px and pushed the first table row to 296px, half the usable height; at 375px the header
    was 178px, the first row started at 344px and **two of seven rows** were visible. The page is a
    list of things to buy, and the list was below the fold.

    No brokerage puts buying power in a card above the instrument list. Fidelity, Schwab, E*Trade and
    Vanguard all render "cash available to trade" as a compact labelled figure in a summary strip, and
    Robinhood keeps it off the list entirely - it belongs where the decision is made, which is the
    order ticket. This app already does that: the order modal shows the fee, the total and the
    balance afterwards. So the figure stays, because a student choosing what to buy wants to see their
    budget while scanning, but as a figure on the header's baseline rather than a card - and the page
    is on components/ui/_page_header like every other page, which it also was not. %>
<div class="w-full">
  <%# @portfolio#trading_off_notice? rather than a bare `!trading_enabled?`, so a student who has
      dismissed this switch-off does not see it again - and so the next switch-off, which stamps a
      newer classrooms.trading_disabled_at, brings it back. %>
  <% if @portfolio&.trading_off_notice? %>
    <%= render layout: "components/ui/callout",
               locals: { title: "Trading is turned off for your classroom",
                         body: "You cannot buy or sell right now. Your teacher can turn it back on.",
                         class: "mb-5" } do %>
      <%= render "portfolios/trading_off_dismiss", portfolio: @portfolio %>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>

  <%# The page states how old its prices are, once, rather than every row repeating the same date. A row only
      says anything when it is behind the freshest price here - see `_index_row`.

      This replaces a sentence about a big move not making a company a better buy, which belonged to the
      Change column that is now gone: the thing it cautioned against is no longer on the screen. What is on
      the screen is a price a student can place an order against, and its age is the fact worth stating. %>
  <% priced_on = @stocks.filter_map(&:last_trading_day).max %>
  <% price_note = if priced_on
                    "Prices are updated once a day, after the market closes. " \
                      "These are as of #{l(priced_on, format: :long)}."
                  else
                    "Prices have not been updated yet."
                  end %>
  <%= render layout: "components/ui/page_header",
             locals: { title: "Trading floor",
                       description: price_note } do %>
    <%# show_holdings? on its own. The `|| current_user.student?` that used to be here widened this
        to students with no persisted portfolio, and stocks#index only sets @portfolio for a student
        who has one - so a student without a portfolio got a 500, not a degraded page. %>
    <% if policy(Stock).show_holdings? %>
      <div class="text-left lg:text-right">
        <p class="text-xs font-medium text-slate-600">Cash to invest</p>
        <p class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 tabular-nums">
          <%= number_to_currency(@portfolio.cash_balance) %>
        </p>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>

  <div class="space-y-6">
    <%# No cadence in this section's description. The page description above states it once, with the date
        the prices actually carry - this said "Prices update every school day", which was a second claim
        about the same thing and a wrong one: the job's cron runs Tuesday to Saturday and knows nothing
        about school terms. %>
    <%# The description addresses whoever is reading, and for staff it also says what the Held by column
        counts. It was one string - "Companies you can buy shares in right now" - shown to teachers and
        admins too, neither of whom can buy anything.

        The scope belongs here rather than in a tooltip or a legend because "2 of 3" is unreadable
        without knowing which three, and the three differ by role: every classroom for an admin, their
        own for a teacher. design.md puts a section's explanation under its heading and above its table,
        in the heading's own column. %>
    <% stocks_description = active_stocks_description(current_user, students: @class_students) %>
    <%= render "stocks/stocks_table",
               title: "Active stocks",
               description: stocks_description,
               stocks: @stocks.active %>

    <%# A stock you hold is listed however long ago it was archived - you must always be able to sell it.
        `listed:` is gone with the disclosure it fed: `Stock::LIST_RETENTION` governed a list of companies
        nobody could act on, and it still governs the admin one. %>
    <%= render "stocks/archived_stocks", stocks: @stocks.archived %>
  </div>
</div>
